        "status": "Contact Oleg Drokin (green@whamcloud.com) with any questions on this account"
      },
      "date": "2026-11-01 05:10:22.000000000",
      "message": "Patch Set 2:\n\n(1 comment)\n\nAI code review results below, please doublecheck\n\nLooks good; the check is reachable on the passive path for any peer connecting from a reserved port, and every in-tree LNetNIInit() caller uses LNET_PID_LUSTRE, so legitimate kernel peers are unaffected. One optional commit-message nit inline, no need to re-spin for it.\n\nOn the open question of whether PID checks are still worth having: beyond LNET_PID_ANY, a kernel-branch HELLO could previously claim a PID with LNET_PID_USERFLAG set. The resulting peer is then treated as a userspace peer, so ksocknal_peer_failed() never notifies LNet that it died and ksocknal_process_receive() rewrites the source of every header. If PIDs are being phased out, forcing recv_id.pid = LNET_PID_LUSTRE and ignoring kshm_src_pid would close the same holes. Rejecting the HELLO, as done here, also points out a misconfigured peer, so either approach seems fine.",
      "accounts_in_message": [],
      "_revision_number": 2
    }
  ],
